## Why This Is Asked

This question tests the ability to design an end-to-end, event-driven AWS workflow that combines storage, NLP classification, and structured indexing. It emphasizes practical decisions about ordering, retries, and privacy controls for handling real customer data.

## Key Concepts

- Event-driven architecture with S3 and Lambda
- Amazon Comprehend for text classification and named entity recognition (NER)
- Output consolidation and DynamoDB indexing
- IAM least privilege, KMS encryption, and data privacy best practices

## Code Example

```javascript
exports.handler = async (event) => {
  // 1. Get S3 object
  const s3Object = await s3.getObject({
    Bucket: event.bucket,
    Key: event.key
  }).promise();

  const ticket = JSON.parse(s3Object.Body);

  // 2. Validate schema
  if (!ticket.ticketId || !ticket.text) {
    throw new Error('Invalid ticket schema');
  }

  // 3. Classify with Comprehend
  const classifyResult = await comprehend.classifyDocument({
    Text: ticket.text,
    EndpointArn: COMPREHEND_ENDPOINT
  }).promise();

  // 4. Extract entities
  const entitiesResult = await comprehend.detectEntities({
    Text: ticket.text,
    LanguageCode: 'en'
  }).promise();

  const topEntities = entitiesResult.Entities
    .sort((a, b) => b.Score - a.Score)
    .slice(0, 3);

  // 5. Store results in S3 (encrypted)
  const output = {
    ticketId: ticket.ticketId,
    category: classifyResult.Classes[0].Name,
    confidence: classifyResult.Classes[0].Score,
    entities: topEntities,
    timestamp: new Date().toISOString()
  };

  await s3.putObject({
    Bucket: OUTPUT_BUCKET,
    Key: `results/${ticket.ticketId}.json`,
    Body: JSON.stringify(output),
    ServerSideEncryption: 'aws:kms'
  }).promise();

  // 6. Upsert DynamoDB
  await dynamoDB.put({
    TableName: TICKETS_TABLE,
    Item: {
      ticketId: ticket.ticketId,
      s3Key: `results/${ticket.ticketId}.json`,
      category: output.category,
      confidence: output.confidence,
      entities: topEntities
    }
  }).promise();

  return { statusCode: 200, body: 'Success' };
};
```

## Architecture Considerations

| Component | Consideration |
|-----------|----------------|
| **S3 Trigger** | Use S3 Event Notifications to trigger Lambda on object creation |
| **Lambda** | Configure timeout (30s+), memory (512MB+), and VPC if needed |
| **IAM** | Least privilege: Lambda needs read on input bucket, write on output bucket, Comprehend and DynamoDB access |
| **Encryption** | Use SSE-KMS for S3 buckets; enable at-rest encryption for DynamoDB |
| **Error Handling** | Implement DLQ for failed processing; add try-catch with exponential backoff for API calls |
| **Cost** | Comprehend pricing is per request; consider batching if high volume |
| **Privacy** | Never log PII; use AWS PrivateLink for Comprehend if required |

## Why This Is Asked
Tests real-world orchestration across AWS AI services, privacy controls, and fault tolerance in a streaming-like ingest path. It probes trade-offs between latency, cost, and compliance when handling PII.

## Key Concepts
- Event-driven orchestration (S3 PUT, Lambda/EventBridge)
- Text analytics (Comprehend: intents, PII)
- Custom model inference (SageMaker endpoint)
- Data redaction and dual-storage (original + redacted) with KMS
- Upsert pattern in DynamoDB with IDs and metadata
- IAM, retries, observability, and cost controls

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal Node.js Lambda handler for the orchestrator
exports.handler = async (event) => {
  const record = event.Records[0];
  const bucket = record.s3.bucket.name;
  const key = record.s3.object.key;

  // 1. Fetch transcript from S3
  const transcript = await s3.getObject({ Bucket: bucket, Key: key }).promise();
  const chatData = JSON.parse(transcript.Body.toString());

  // 2. Extract text for analysis
  const chatText = chatData.messages.map(m => m.content).join(' ');

  // 3. Comprehend analysis
  const [comprehendResult, piiResult] = await Promise.all([
    comprehend.detectDominantLanguage({ Text: chatText }).promise(),
    comprehend.detectPiiEntities({ Text: chatText, LanguageCode: 'en' }).promise()
  ]);

  // 4. SageMaker risk classification
  const riskResponse = await sageMakerRuntime.invokeEndpoint({
    EndpointName: process.env.RISK_ENDPOINT,
    ContentType: 'application/json',
    Body: JSON.stringify({ text: chatText, metadata: chatData.metadata })
  }).promise();

  const riskClassification = JSON.parse(riskResponse.Body.toString());

  // 5. Redact PII and store both versions
  const redactedText = await redactPii(chatText, piiResult.Entities);
  const [originalKey, redactedKey] = await Promise.all([
    storeTranscript(chatData, 'original'),
    storeTranscript({...chatData, text: redactedText}, 'redacted')
  ]);

  // 6. Upsert DynamoDB item
  await dynamodb.putItem({
    TableName: process.env.CHAT_TABLE,
    Item: {
      chatId: chatData.id,
      userHash: chatData.userId,
      language: comprehendResult.Languages[0].LanguageCode,
      riskScore: riskClassification.score,
      originalKey,
      redactedKey,
      detectedIntents: comprehendResult.Intents
    }
  }).promise();
};
```
